`FFPlay` wrapper class wraps around SubprocessExecutor to run video player asynchronously.

Example usage:

```c++
using namespace std::chrono_literals;
FFPlay ffplay("/path/to/ffplay", std::make_unique<SubprocessExecutor>());

ffplay.width(500)
  ->height(500)
  ->start(10s)
  ->duration(1m + 30s)
  ->OpenPlayer("/path/to/video.mp4");

// Plays video.mp4 in 500x500 window, starting from t=10s, ending at t=1m 40s.
// In the meantime you can do anything such as block to get user input
cin >> something;

ffplay.ClosePlayer(1000);  // Close the player with 1000ms timeout. If timeout expires, force close it.
```

* Subprocess executor now also throws error if try to `Wait()` without starting a new running process
* Added MockSubprocessExecutor to help test ffplay and other future classes which run subprocesses.
* Fixes #13
